// MEASXY.QS0 - measuring in the X and Y bases, plus mid-circuit reset.
//
// Demonstrates: MEASURE_X, MEASURE_Y, RESET, classical control (IF).
//
// Qubit-level explanation:
//   H 0 creates the state |+> = (|0>+|1>)/sqrt2 on qubit 0. |+> is an
//   eigenstate of X with eigenvalue +1, so measuring it in the X basis
//   (MEASURE_X) is deterministic: the OUTCOME c0 is always 0. This is the
//   X-basis analogue of measuring |0> in the Z basis.
//
//   S 1 then H 1 creates |+i> = (|0>+i|1>)/sqrt2 on qubit 1, the +1
//   eigenstate of Y. Measuring in the Y basis (MEASURE_Y) is therefore also
//   deterministic: the outcome c1 is always 0.
//
//   X 2 prepares |1> on qubit 2, then RESET 2 collapses it back to |0>.
//   RESET is "measure, then correct if the outcome was 1"; no classical bit
//   is written.
//
//   The IF block is dead code here because c0 is always 0, but it shows how
//   a measurement outcome can drive later gates.
//
//   Key point: the post-measurement state of qubit 0 is still |+>, and that
//   of qubit 1 is still |+i>. Both are superpositions in the Z basis, so a
//   later Z-basis sample reports qubits 0 and 1 as random. The deterministic
//   quantities are the X/Y measurement OUTCOMES (c0 and c1 are always 0),
//   not the Z-basis histogram.
//
//   The histogram is therefore |000>, |001>, |010>, |011> at ~25% each,
//   with qubit 2 always |0> after the reset.
//
// Run:
//   plasma run examples/MEASXY.QS0 --shots 1000
QUBITS 3
H 0
S 1
H 1
X 2
RESET 2
MEASURE_X 0 -> c0
MEASURE_Y 1 -> c1
IF c0 == 1 THEN
  X 2
ENDIF
SHOT 1000
PRINT
END